const WAVE3_RECEIPT = [{"group": "concurrency", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-concurrency.md", "must_count": 23, "conflicts_resolved": ["Virtual threads vs Process.onExit() for the SDK's async path — map conflict 13 said 'virtual threads, no reactive stack'; the process dive says onExit()'s CompletableFuture. Resolved for the process dive: conflict 13 rejected a reactive runtime, it did not mandate Thread.ofVirtual(); onExit() is JDK 9 and free on the floor of 17, while virtual threads would force 17 → 21 for a fan-out this one-command-at-a-time SDK never performs. JAVA-CONC-01/02 still govern wherever the fleet does fan out.", "The --enable-preview grep scope — the process dive's check ('grep StructuredTaskScope|--enable-preview over build files must be empty') is over-broad and would flag micronaut-core's test-runtime jvmArg, the corpus's only precedent, as a violation. Resolved in favour of the VT dive's sourceset-scoped check: read which task the flag feeds; a compilerArgs entry on a published main sourceset is the violation, a Test{jvmArgs} entry is not. Same failure shape as the wave-2 correction to GRADLE-DEP-05.", "'No lint exists for GlobalScope or swallowed CancellationException' (the KT-CORO brief's working assumption) vs detekt shipping both — resolved: both ship, and both are active:false by default, so 'detekt is configured' is not 'these checks run'. Re-measured across all 32 clones 2026-09-12: 1/32 activates any (detekt itself, GlobalCoroutineUsage only), 0/32 activate SuspendFunSwallowedCancellation or SuspendFunInFinallySection. Promoted to KT-CORO-01, the gate row.", "GlobalScope: absolute ban vs named exception — detekt's GlobalCoroutineUsage flags both forms identically, and map row M-U-01's @OptIn(DelicateCoroutinesApi) grep is present on both too, so neither is a sufficient check. Resolved: the bare no-argument form is the MUST-fix; GlobalScope.launch(explicitParentContext, …) at an adapter boundary is structurally parented (ktor CIOEngine.kt:73) and is accepted. A finding is a question, never an automatic rewrite.", "synchronized vs ReentrantLock — the widely-trained unconditional 'migrate to ReentrantLock to avoid pinning' formulation is provably wrong above JDK 24, where JEP 491 itself quotes JCIP §13.4 for defaulting to synchronized and says migrated code need not be reverted. Resolved as a floor-conditional rule with the split named at 24.", "'limitedParallelism(n) views do not sum' read as 'do not create views' — resolved: creating views is cheap and correct (a LimitedDispatcher queues its own tasks onto IO's shared pool), the anti-pattern is unbounded blocking work on bare Dispatchers.IO. KT-CORO-06 targets the base dispatcher, not the view count.", "Can runTest's virtual time hide a real race (the corpus's open Kotlin-testing question)? Resolved: yes, structurally — every TestDispatcher runs its queue one task at a time on a single scheduler thread, so a genuine data race or lock-ordering bug cannot fail there. A thread-safety claim needs a separate real-dispatcher massiveRun-shaped stress test; runTest stays default for suspension-order and cancellation logic.", "The basis for the 'no StructuredTaskScope in shipped code' rule — API instability (map conflict 15) vs JEP 12's class-file mechanism (VT dive §6). Resolved to the more specific formulation: minor_version all-16-bits-set pins a preview class file to the exact feature release that compiled it, so a JDK-26-built jar will not run on JDK 27 even with --enable-preview re-passed. That makes the rule a technical constraint, not a maturity opinion."], "needs_another_round": ["JAVA-CONC unresearched rows (M-N-05..M-N-11) — which of CPU-vs-I/O regime split, ExecutorService shutdown/close semantics, CompletableFuture terminal operations, double-checked locking, SimpleDateFormat statics, @GuardedBy documentation and the ReentrantReadWriteLock 65,536-reader ceiling earn a rule row, given Error Prone and SpotBugs already catch four of them mechanically — i.e. which are rules and which are JAVA-LINT configuration?", "Kotlin Flow (M-U-07, M-U-10) — does Flow need its own depth section covering catch-operator placement and the StateFlow conflation contract, or do two rows in coroutines.md cover it? Both were explicitly out of the coroutines dive's scope.", "JDK 27 GA re-verification — JDK 27 went GA 2026-09-15, three days after this file; did JEP 533's shape (R_X type parameter, timeout(), awaitAll() removed) ship unchanged, and is structured concurrency still targeted at JDK 28 for finalisation? JAVA-CONC-05/06 must be re-read against the GA javadoc.", "detekt activation defaults — do GlobalCoroutineUsage, SuspendFunSwallowedCancellation, SuspendFunInFinallySection and CoroutineLaunchedInTestWithoutRunTest stay active:false in the first stable release cut from the dev.detekt.* main branch? KT-CORO-01's whole premise depends on it."]}, "rule_id_count": 27, "dives": [{"slug": "java-virtual-threads-and-structured-concurrency", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-concurrency/java-virtual-threads-and-structured-concurrency.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["jackson-core changed its default BufferRecyclerPool from threadLocalPool() to a lock-free pool in 2.17.0, then reverted the default back in 2.17.1 after production regressions, and deprecated the lock-free pool entirely in 2.18.0 — the 'obvious' virtual-thread fix for ThreadLocal caching was tried by the flagship library itself and rolled back.", "HikariCP maintainers left two independent PRs (2023, 2024) to replace internal synchronized with ReentrantLock for virtual-thread friendliness unmerged, and an open issue shows a real production report of JEP 491's documented 'still pins' class-initializer case on JDK 24.", "Preview status is not just an API-stability warning: JEP 12's class-file mechanism means preview bytecode only loads on the exact JDK feature release that compiled it, with --enable-preview required again at runtime — a published SDK built against a preview API cannot be consumed cross-JDK at all, which is a much harder constraint than 'the API might change'.", "fork() returning Subtask instead of Future is not a recent change — it's been true since the very first preview in JDK 21 (JEP 453), years before the brief's other version-sensitive changes.", "micronaut-core's sole --enable-preview usage in the whole 32-repo corpus is scoped to the Test task's JVM args only, not the JavaCompile task for its main sourceset — the one corpus precedent for preview flags isn't even a production compile-time usage.", "StructuredTaskScope became a sealed interface (not an abstract class) as early as JEP 525 (JDK 26), a detail rarely mentioned in older tutorials still describing it as a class with subclasses ShutdownOnFailure/ShutdownOnSuccess."]}, {"slug": "kotlin-coroutines-correctness", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-concurrency/kotlin-coroutines-correctness.md", "sources_count": 14, "surprises": ["detekt already ships lint coverage for two of the brief's five MUST rows — GlobalCoroutineUsage (GlobalScope) and SuspendFunSwallowedCancellation — but both are active:false by default, so 'detekt is configured' does not mean these checks run; the brief's 'no lint exists' premise only holds for the missing-cooperative-cancellation-check row.", "The exemplar corpus supplies a concrete, real-world instance of the GlobalScope exception the brief asks to decide: ktor's CIOEngine calls GlobalScope.launch(parentContext, ...), passing the caller's real Job so the coroutine is not actually detached — this is the shape a 'named exception' rule should codify.", "Medium blocks every fetch method available (WebFetch, curl+UA, web.archive.org, freedium.cfd) with 403/DNS failures for both Elizarov design-rationale articles; had to fall back to search-engine-indexed verbatim excerpts, flagged explicitly in Sources as partial-confidence.", "detekt also ships SuspendFunInFinallySection (inactive by default) and SleepInsteadOfDelay (active by default) that directly cover two more of this dive's normative candidates (NonCancellable-in-finally, and Thread.sleep on any dispatcher) — neither was anticipated by the brief.", "detekt's coroutines rule classes now live under package dev.detekt.rules.coroutines rather than the historical io.gitlab.arturbosch.detekt.rules.coroutines, a rename that will break an agent's training-era assumptions about the group/package coordinates for custom detekt config."]}, {"slug": "process-and-cli-boundary", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-concurrency/process-and-cli-boundary.md", "sources_count": 15, "surprises": ["The JVM has no killpg/process-group primitive at all — Process.destroy()/destroyForcibly() only ever reach the immediate child, so a kill ladder for a CLI that forks its own children needs an explicit Process.descendants() walk that ocx-sdk-python's POSIX process-group approach doesn't need to replicate", "On Windows, Process.destroy() is already forcible (TerminateProcess) — there is no graceful-shutdown phase at all on that platform, so the entire grace-window rationale of the kill ladder is POSIX-only", "java.net.http ships zero JSON support of any kind (BodyHandlers only return String/InputStream/Path) — the zero-runtime-dependency commitment's hand-rolled JSON reader isn't a corner being cut, it's the only option the JDK leaves open for parsing either HTTP bodies or captured CLI stdout", "The 32-repo exemplar corpus has no exemplar at all for this row (M-Y-02 is 'partial' coverage sourced only from ocx-sdk-python) — the OCX SDK itself will be the fleet's first measured instance of this pattern, so every normative rule here is evidenced against prior art and JDK docs rather than a corpus library already doing it", "Choosing virtual threads for this one boundary alone would force the SDK's JDK floor from the map's default 17 up to 21 — a four-major-version jump paid for a concurrency regime (high fan-out blocking-style I/O) this 'one command at a time' SDK never actually exercises"]}]}, {"group": "gradle-settings", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-settings.md", "must_count": 9, "conflicts_resolved": ["A — Dive: 'no corpus repo violates the duplicate-leaf-name ban.' Re-measured: three do (sqldelight settings.gradle:62,67 and testcontainers settings.gradle:30-31 are live instances, each with a uniform explicit group; nowinandroid settings.gradle.kts:69-79 has the most duplicates but assigns no group and is correct). Resolved toward measurement, and GRADLE-STRUCT-10 gains the uniform-group conjunct because Gradle's default group is the dotted parent path (Project.java:393-394).", "B — Dive: subproject gradle.properties 'not observed in the corpus', verified by `find . -mindepth 2 -name gradle.properties` as a MUST-fail. Re-measured: 17/32 repos have one and zero are violations — every non-root file carrying an org.gradle.* key is a build root (build-logic, buildSrc, includeBuild, sample, docs snippet), nowinandroid's even citing gradle/gradle#2534 as the reason. Resolved: the dive's verification is discarded; GRADLE-STRUCT-11 is scoped to org.gradle.* keys outside a build root, and the general best-practice half is deliberately not shipped.", "C — Dive lists diffplug/spotless under 'no gate at all'. Read in full, spotless@dc2a4cb9a3:settings.gradle:32-46 is the corpus's cleanest credential gate (push = true only inside `if (user != null && pass != null)`). Resolved toward the source: spotless moves from violator to exemplar and becomes the portable HttpBuildCache template.", "D — Dive: 'no checked-out settings.gradle* gates push on more than CI-ness' — a grep-scope artifact. Extending to **/*.settings.gradle.kts adds ktor (ktorsettings.develocity.settings.gradle.kts:75, isPush = isCIRun, a second violator inside a reusable Settings plugin) and junit-framework (junitbuild.settings-conventions.settings.gradle.kts:50, pushEnabled defaultValue = false, a third satisfier). Resolved: the verification glob changes; tally becomes 3 satisfy / 2 violate.", "E — Dive and GRADLE-STRUCT-02 both say gradle/gradle is the only (1/32) build-logic-settings split. Re-measured 3/32: ktor's build-settings-logic (pluginManagement-included, only *.settings.gradle.kts — the better copy-target for the OCX plugin) and junit-framework's gradle/base (a partial split, one Settings plugin leaked into gradle/plugins). Corrects jvm-gradle-core.md verdict 7's claim that junit ships the merged form; GRADLE-STRUCT-02 stays SHOULD since the cost argument is unchanged."], "needs_another_round": ["Settings-plugin functional testing — how does GradleRunner apply a Plugin<Settings> to a test project, and does the compileOnly/withPluginClasspath incompatibility (GRADLE-PLUG-14) behave the same at settings scope? Every GRADLE-PLUG-22..28 rule is currently verified by reading source because nothing established this.", "Cold-runner cost of execution-time provisioning — what does GRADLE-PLUG-23's first-task-pays download cost on a GitHub-hosted runner versus a setup-ocx-style CI step, and does that change whether the Gradle plugin should provision at all when a CI action already did?", "Latent-collision detection for GRADLE-STRUCT-10 — is there a Gradle-side way (resolution-result walk, build scan dependency view, dependencies report diff) to detect a project that actually lost conflict resolution, so the rule can report a real defect rather than a latent one?"]}, "rule_id_count": 10, "dives": [{"slug": "settings-time-surface-and-tool-provisioning", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-gradle-settings/settings-time-surface-and-tool-provisioning.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["Gradle's own docs say registering a shared BuildService via gradle.sharedServices.registerIfAbsent(...) is explicitly 'safe with Isolated Projects' -- the opposite of the brief's framing that this shape is IP-hostile by construction; the safety is bounded because two of IP's four constraint categories (cross-build, build-to-project access) are still 'not fully enforced yet' as of 9.7.0", "JavaToolchainResolver itself extends BuildService<BuildServiceParameters.None>, and JavaToolchainDownload carries only a bare HTTPS URI to a zip/tar -- there is no checksum field anywhere in the toolchain-resolver contract, so even for genuine JDK provisioning Gradle's sanctioned seat never enforces a hash", "gradle/gradle#847 (duplicate subproject leaf names) is still open and reproducing on Gradle 8.12.1 as of a 2025-06-30 comment, with zero fix across the 8.x or 9.x lines despite 137 reactions since 2016", "No corpus settings file gates remote build-cache push on credential possession except testcontainers-java; detekt's seemingly-careful isCiBuild-only gate is not actually fork-PR-safe, since fork PRs also run under CI", "Kafka's build-scan gate (publishing.onlyIf { it.authenticated }) is a different Develocity feature (build scan, not cache) that just happens to be the map-cited exemplar shape -- its own buildCache{} block sidesteps the push question by disabling remote push outright rather than gating it", "JSpecify defaulting to strict/errors in Kotlin (since 2.1) while JSR-305 stays warn-only makes the JSpecify migration the compatibility-risk direction, exactly backwards from an agent's likely instinct that adopting the newer spec-blessed annotation set is the safe upgrade"]}]}, {"group": "publishing", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-publishing.md", "must_count": 14, "conflicts_resolved": ["maven-publish x configuration cache severity: central-portal-and-provenance rates the literal-credentials ban SHOULD because Gradle 9 degrades gracefully; raised to MUST (GRADLE-PUB-08) — its own rationale admits no case where the literal is better, Gradle 8.x still hard-fails, and a literal credential in a committed build file is a secret in VCS. Also closes GRADLE-CACHE-08's open question as 'partially incompatible, exactly one config shape'.", "GRADLE-DIST-13's rationale ('Gradle does not make this free; kafka needed a hand-rolled pom.withXml') is wrong: Shadow's shadow-configuration -> RUNTIME-scope POM mapping dates to 1.1.0 (2014-08-26) and kafka's reason is the coupled manifest Class-Path write (shadow#324), not a POM defect. Primary docs + changelog + the issue title outrank an inference from reading kafka's build file. DIST-13's rule survives; the mechanism becomes GRADLE-PUB-11 and DIST-13's rationale needs correcting at authoring time.", "OIDC 'trusted publishing' at the Central Portal: config-inventory.md Axis 3 asserts it exists post-OSSRH-sunset; central-portal-and-provenance fetched the Portal guide, requirements, Publisher API and registration pages on 2026-09-12 and found no OIDC path. Primary-source negative beats an unsourced analogy to PyPI — the OCX SDK uses a Portal token in CI secrets and the claim goes to Open questions, not into a rule.", "mavenLocal() MUST NOT (GRADLE-PUB-15) collided with the consumability probe that resolves through it (GRADLE-PUB-13). Resolved by carve-out written into PUB-15's verification cell rather than by weakening either: PUB-15 governs a Gradle consuming build's repositories { }; the probe is a Maven module whose purpose is resolving a deliberately-unpublished artifact, and mvn reads ~/.m2 by default rather than through a declared Gradle repository.", "Publishing-plugin choice: the corpus majority hand-rolls raw maven-publish (18/32, 7 with no higher-level plugin) against the recommendation of nmcp/vanniktech. Resolved by naming the departure rather than following the count — the majority pattern predates the configuration cache and Isolated Projects, and nmcp documents compatibility with both by construction; codifying the majority would codify debt.", "Sigstore vs provenance severity, both at 0/32 corpus adoption: split on whether a consumer can act on the artifact. Central validates Sigstore bundles (since 2025-01-28) so a bundle is verifiable — SHOULD. Nothing Portal-side consumes a GitHub provenance attestation — CONSIDER."], "needs_another_round": ["Central Portal authentication and the Gradle-side SNAPSHOT path — does the Portal support OIDC/tokenless publishing (config-inventory asserts yes, primary sources are silent), and what is the snapshot equivalent of central-publishing-maven-plugin >= 0.7.0 for nmcp and vanniktech? Neither dive covered a Gradle snapshot channel at all.", "The 42-file mavenLocal() census — which repos, and in which is the declaration inside a committed consuming repositories { } (the finding) versus a gitignored local override or a consumability probe (the carve-out)? GRADLE-PUB-15 currently has a count but no named violator.", "Release-gate ordering for the jvm-release skill — at which point does the binary-compatibility gate run relative to sign/checksum/upload, and can a failed VALIDATED deployment be dropped without burning the version number? Immutability makes the ordering load-bearing and no dive established it.", "Shadow's manifest Class-Path coupling — did GradleUp/shadow#324's 2025-01-24 closure change the shadow configuration's Class-Path write, or was it closed 'working as intended, use a different configuration name'? GRADLE-PUB-11's exception clause depends on the answer and the comment thread was never read.", "version-catalog plugin publishing (map row M-H-08) — 1/32 (ktor), deferred by the map to this group and answered by neither dive; does the OCX Gradle plugin ship a catalog for consumers, which is what would earn it a rule rather than a MAY-level mention?"]}, "rule_id_count": 21, "dives": [{"slug": "central-portal-and-provenance", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-publishing/central-portal-and-provenance.md", "sources_count": 19, "surprises": ["The two Gradle doc pages the brief named for mavenLocal() (declaring_repositories.html, best_practices_dependencies.html) say NOTHING about it — the actual warning ('restricted to prototyping rather than production builds') lives on a third, unnamed page: declaring_repositories_basics.html", "The maven-publish/config-cache question resolves cleanly: gradle/gradle#33830 (merged 2025-06-13, milestone 9.0.0 RC1) changed the failure mode from a hard build failure (Gradle 8.x) to silent config-cache disablement (Gradle 9.0+) for exactly one shape — literal inline credentials{} — not a broad plugin incompatibility", "0/32 exemplars use any git-tag-driven version plugin at all, which makes the ocx-sdk-python-style tag-vs-version CI check strictly necessary rather than a nice-to-have, since nothing else in observed practice enforces that consistency", "Central's own placeholder-jar fallback for sources/javadoc (a jar containing just a README) is documented and sanctioned, not an anti-pattern to flag", "3/32 exemplars still reference oss.sonatype.org URLs nearly a year after OSSRH's 2025-06-30 shutdown — live evidence of the exact AI-hallucination trap the corpus itself preserves", "Sonatype's own Sigstore announcement states 'we have no intention of replacing PGP signatures' at launch, while also leaving the door open to eventually requiring both — there is no scheduled PGP deprecation to warn about"]}, {"slug": "consumability-across-build-systems", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-publishing/consumability-across-build-systems.md", "sources_count": 15, "surprises": ["DIST-13's premise was wrong: Shadow's shadow-configuration-to-POM mapping isn't new in 9.0.0 — it dates to Shadow 1.1.0 (2014); what's actually new in 9.0.0 is the shadowRuntimeElements GMM variant.", "kafka's hand-rolled pom.withXml isn't working around a Shadow POM defect at all — it deliberately avoids Shadow's built-in `shadow` configuration because that configuration ALSO writes the same deps into the manifest Class-Path header (GradleUp/shadow#324), which kafka doesn't want.", "kafka disables GenerateModuleMetadata entirely for its shaded :clients module — a real, previously unrecorded exemplar answering the OCX SDK's 'suppress GMM or not' decision.", "GradleUp/shadow#324 (cited by kafka's own comment as the reason for its POM patch) is actually about the JAR manifest Class-Path header, not about POM dependency correctness — a stale/misleading-looking citation that still turns out to be internally consistent once read in full.", "junit-framework's own test suite (MavenPomFileTests.java) asserts the literal `<!-- do_not_remove: published-with-gradle-metadata -->` marker text as a golden-line snapshot — direct corpus proof of the Gradle-docs claim, not just a docs paraphrase.", "Maven 4's 'build POM vs consumer POM' isn't a new capability at all — it's Maven 4 first-classing what flatten-maven-plugin already did in Maven 3, now opt-in via maven.consumer.pom.flatten (still RC, unshipped)."]}]}, {"group": "bazel-java", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-bazel-java.md", "must_count": 12, "conflicts_resolved": ["ijar vs turbine, between the two dives — the toolchains dive says the default toolchain's header compiler is turbine and 'ijar-based' is a misdescription; the deps/Kotlin dive says ijar strips Kotlin inline-function bodies. Resolved by scope split: turbine parses SOURCE for java_library/kt_jvm_library, ijar strips a PREBUILT jar for java_import. Neither rule cures the other's failure — forcibly_disable_header_compilation does not fix the Kotlin trap, and routing Kotlin through maven.install does not change the header compiler. Both survive as separate rules (BZL-JAVA-07, BZL-JAVA-18) plus one merged failure mode.", "Strict-deps severity and spelling — the toolchains dive made 'confirm the flag name on your own pinned binary' a MUST whose verification cannot run without a bazel binary (this program has none, and an adopting agent often will not either). Resolved by splitting: BZL-JAVA-05 demoted to SHOULD with the exemplar-evidenced spelling (--experimental_strict_java_deps, rules_jvm_external@449754dcbb:.bazelrc:9) as the writable default, and the MUST moved to BZL-JAVA-04, the misreading that licenses a real edit (absent flag already means error, so 'not enforced here' ends in =off).", "strict_visibility severity — the deps dive rated it CONSIDER on 'absent is a finding to raise, not an automatic block'. Promoted to SHOULD (BZL-JAVA-13) on re-measurement: three of the four exemplars carrying a real external Maven graph set it (bazel@948b8c70e2:MODULE.bazel:269, grpc-java@fc4314419d:MODULE.bazel:78,100, rules_jvm_external@449754dcbb:MODULE.bazel:86), and it is the only mechanism that fails an undeclared transitive use at the point of the edit.", "fail_if_repin_required — who actually violates. The deps dive's exemplar table named only dagger (satisfies) and grpc-java (violates by absence), implying the careful repos set it. Re-measured here: bazel-contrib__rules_jvm_external@449754dcbb:MODULE.bazel:81-86 — the ruleset that SHIPS the flag sets lock_file and strict_visibility on its own primary maven.install and not the gate (it does set it on a test fixture at :506-507). MUST stands; the violation row is now named.", "M-X-08 / _deploy.jar — the toolchains dive deferred it wholly to jvm-distribution.md DIST-02..DIST-11 and emitted no BZL-JAVA rule. Resolved: the shading taxonomy is correctly inherited, but there is Bazel-only residue that file cannot carry — per bazel.build/reference/be/java, name_deploy.jar is an implicit output 'only built if explicitly requested', so a green bazel build //... never builds the shipped artifact. Ships as BZL-JAVA-21, MUST for CLI/application consumers.", "x_lambdas and dagger — the deps dive filed dagger's unset x_lambdas under 'silent-default territory… not a counterexample'. Sharpened to a confirmed violation: dagger-compiler/main/java/dagger/internal/codegen/kotlin/BUILD:22-28 globs *.kt through compat_kt_jvm_library (tools/bazel_compat.bzl:20-26 → rules_kotlin's kt_jvm_library) while dagger-compiler/build.gradle.kts:6-10 applies kotlinJvm over source dirs wired to the same main/java tree (buildSrc/src/main/kotlin/dagger/gradle/build/DaggerConventionPlugin.kt:158-168). Same .kt files, two build systems, zero x_lambdas/kt_kotlinc_options anywhere in the repo.", "Line-number drift in the toolchains dive's dagger citations — it cites Error Prone at .bazelrc:11-13, the four version flags at :17-28 and --add-exports at :26. Re-read at the pinned SHA: :17-18, :22-25, :28 (-source/-target) and :34. The pub audit (Axis 4) had it right. All dive citations corrected in the file and the correction recorded inline, because a grep written from the dive's numbers misses. The dive's other three .bazelrc citations (bazel :70-73, rules_java :4-7, rules_jvm_external :3-10 and :9) were re-read and are correct."], "needs_another_round": ["Bazel-Java testing and coverage — what is the minimum wiring that makes `bazel test //...` actually run a JUnit 5 suite and emit a coverage report a JAVA-TEST floor can read? rules_java ships no first-party JUnit 5 rule, contrib_rules_jvm's java_junit5_test is the only path, and coverage_report_generator is customised by 0/6 exemplars. The sibling testing.md owns sizing and `manual` tags, not JUnit-platform wiring — a real hole between two files, not a duplicate.", "java_export and publishing to Maven Central from Bazel — does rules_jvm_external's java_export/maven_publish produce a Central-Portal-acceptable bundle (sources jar, javadoc jar, per-file GPG signature, complete POM), or is Bazel a non-publisher that always hands off to Gradle or Maven? Load-bearing for the OCX SDK: the jvm-release skill assumes a Gradle or Maven publisher, and dagger — the corpus's canonical-Bazel repo — keeps Gradle precisely for publishing packaging.", "Worker-state reproducibility for KotlinCompile — BZL-JAVA-20 is a CONSIDER because no primary source states a correctness defect and no exemplar overrides the default. One experiment (build twice with and without --strategy=KotlinCompile=local, diff the jars) would either promote it to a SHOULD with evidence or retire it to prose. Below the line for this program; promote only if an adopter reports a Kotlin flake."]}, "rule_id_count": 23, "dives": [{"slug": "bazel-java-toolchains-and-compilation", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-bazel-java/bazel-java-toolchains-and-compilation.md", "sources_count": 16, "surprises": ["Bazel's own docs disagree on the strict-deps flag's name: command-line-reference calls it --experimental_strict_java_deps, user-manual calls it --strict_java_deps with no prefix — could not be resolved against a real bazel binary (none installed in the sandbox).", "Strict Java deps is not something you turn on: per user-manual, default/strict/error are the same behavior and that IS the behavior when the flag is unset — an absent strict-deps line does not mean unenforced.", "Header compilation on the default toolchain is turbine (TurbineDirect), not ijar — bazel-and-java's own prose only names ijar in that section, which misdescribes the mechanism (ijar strips a compiled jar; turbine parses source directly, no javac back-end invoked).", "google/error-prone ships literally no MODULE.bazel/WORKSPACE/BUILD file at all; it's wired into Bazel purely via JavaBuilder + --javacopt/java_package_configuration, never as a dependency.", "bazel-and-java's own page states the 'default javac options' are -source 8 -target 8, directly contradicting its own (and user-manual's) stated --java_language_version default of 11 two paragraphs apart — an internal doc inconsistency, not a live default.", "contrib_rules_jvm's lint wrappers ARE bzlmod-native (own MODULE.bazel uses a `linter` module extension), but zero of the 32 program exemplars adopt them or apple_rules_lint at all."]}, {"slug": "bazel-java-external-deps-and-kotlin", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-bazel-java/bazel-java-external-deps-and-kotlin.md", "sources_count": 16, "surprises": ["No merge between bazel-contrib/rules_jvm_external and bazel-contrib/rules_jvm has happened (both remain live, separate, non-archived repos) — but rules_kotlin itself underwent a GitHub org transfer from bazelbuild/ to bazel-contrib/, which is likely what the recent-shifts scout half-detected.", "rules_jvm_external's freshness gate is NOT unconditional like crate_universe's: fail_if_repin_required defaults to False (confirmed in coursier.bzl source), so a stale or even hand-corrupted maven_install.json is merely a printed warning and the build still succeeds unless that flag is explicitly set — the opposite failure-mode shape from BZL-RUST-01.", "The lock file self-checks on two independent hash fields, not one: __INPUT_ARTIFACTS_HASH (declared-vs-lock drift) and __RESOLVED_ARTIFACTS_HASH (lock-body self-tamper/bad-merge check) — both gated by the same fail_if_repin_required flag.", "The ijar/Kotlin-inline-function bug is ancient (filed and fixed in 2019, PR #69) — there is no live rules_jvm_external version lacking the fix, so no version-floor caveat is actually needed for a repo starting today.", "dagger already does almost everything right: fail_if_repin_required=True and lock_file are both set (MODULE.bazel:185-186), yet it still never sets version_conflict_policy or x_lambdas — showing the gate/pin discipline and the conflict-policy/codegen discipline are adopted completely independently in practice.", "No exemplar in the corpus (0/6 Bazel-carrying repos, including dagger's dual Gradle+Bazel Kotlin build) sets x_lambdas at all — the silent-default bytecode divergence is not hypothetical, it's the observed universal state."]}]}, {"group": "maven-and-ant", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-maven-and-ant.md", "must_count": 15, "conflicts_resolved": ["jackson-databind's `argLine`: the dive recorded `FasterXML__jackson-databind@a906e1782b:pom.xml:44` as SATISFYING the JaCoCo-composition rule. Re-measured this consolidation — it VIOLATES it. `:44-46` is two bare `--add-opens` flags with no `@{argLine}` placeholder while `:212-215` binds jacoco-maven-plugin; the POM carries its own smoking gun at `:51-54` (`<jacocoStrict>false</jacocoStrict>`, comment \"For some reason, release fails due to missing jacoco... so disable for now\") and a consequently non-failing `requireFilesExist` on `target/jacoco.exec` at `:226-243`. The corpus's counterexample, not its exemplar.", "`mvn verify` vs `install`: the dive stated a flat prohibition (candidate #2, carried from map row M-J-10 unmeasured). Measured the 5 Maven-primary repos' CI this consolidation: `google__guava@5fb424c43a:.github/workflows/ci.yml:53,56` runs a deliberate `-DskipTests install` reactor bootstrap and then gates on `verify` — correct. Resolved: the rule binds the GATE, not every invocation. The real violation is `google__error-prone@c1f99ad5d3:.github/workflows/ci.yml:88,91` (`mvn install -DskipTests` then `mvn test`), which never reaches `verify` at all.", "Surefire version: map row M-J-08 says \"3.6.0-M1\", the dive's candidate #7 quotes the doc banner \"3.6.0 / Last Published 2026-08-31\", frame correction 38 says Central GA tops at 3.5.6. Resolved in favour of Central: MVN-BUILD-05 pins from `maven-metadata.xml` and describes the single-provider consolidation as a 3.6.0-line change not yet GA — a rule quoting the banner instructs an agent to pin a milestone as stable.", "Family routing for the supply-chain rows: the dive routed them to MVN-DEP (it was commissioned to close jvm-dependencies.md's open \"Maven-side checksum and verification policy\" question). But the map routes MVN-DEP to `dependencies.md`, triggered by \"adding a dependency\"; an agent editing `.mvn/maven.config` or `maven-wrapper.properties` is editing build configuration. Resolved: they land in MVN-BUILD (family follows file kind); MVN-DEP-01…11 stand unchanged and are cited, not duplicated.", "Internal tension in the dive: candidate #12 says Maven checksums are never a security control, candidate #14 makes the wrapper checksum a supply-chain MUST. Resolved by scope, both survive — Resolver checksums verify an artifact fetched inside a chain GPG can also cover (integrity only), whereas the wrapper checksum pins a distribution downloaded and executed before any trust chain exists and where no signature is ever checked, so integrity is the only available control there.", "`.mvn/extensions.xml` is 0/32 per the shape audit §1 — but `apache__maven@ea4a417bd2:.github/workflows/maven.yml:77,93-95` copies one in before the Maven-3 leg and swaps it to `~/.m2/extensions.xml` for the Maven-4 leg. Resolved: the census counted committed files; an extension can replace the local repository wholesale, so MVN-BUILD-15 requires reading CI configs, not only `git ls-tree`.", "Ant severity: map decision 3 folds Ant into a depth file because recognise-and-migrate fails rule-distillation's \"it changes a diff\" test; the dive proposes MUST-grade rules. Resolved: MVN-ANT rows ship as conditional-entry rules that fire only when a `build.xml` is in the diff. MVN-ANT-02 earns a MUST at 0/32 adoption because its blast radius is whole-build configuration-cache loss on the *Gradle* side, not on Ant."], "needs_another_round": ["Maven compilation under modern JDKs — does a modern JDK disable implicit annotation processing by default, and must a Maven build set `<maven.compiler.proc>full</maven.compiler.proc>` for processors to run at all? `apache__maven@ea4a417bd2:pom.xml:135` sets it; no dive covered the javac mechanism, and a wrong rule makes an agent believe processors ran when they did not. Highest-value gap in the family.", "Maven toolchains — what is the Maven-side equivalent of the toolchain-vs-`--release` split JAVA-PLAT owns for Gradle: `~/.m2/toolchains.xml`, `maven-toolchains-plugin`, or the `setup-java` matrix? `google__guava@5fb424c43a:.github/workflows/ci.yml:53,56` passes `-Dtoolchain.skip` and `-Dsurefire.toolchain.version`, implying a live toolchain configuration no audit read.", "`maven-antrun-plugin` — Ant inside Maven: what does a rule say about `<target>`/`<tasks>` blocks embedded in a POM, the one Ant surface that appears in live Maven builds rather than legacy files? The map's Ant brief named the plugin; the dive never fetched it, and MVN-ANT as written only fires on a `build.xml`.", "Maven build extensions as a resolution mechanism — does MVN-BUILD-15 become a MUST, and what does a rule say about an extension that replaces the local repository for CI only (apache/maven's Mimir)? Measured 0/32 committed, which is exactly why the CI-injected case was missed."]}, "rule_id_count": 21, "dives": [{"slug": "maven4-lifecycle-and-plugins", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-maven-and-ant/maven4-lifecycle-and-plugins.md", "sources_count": 26, "surprises": ["assertj's `.mvn/maven.config` checksum flags are Maven-Resolver-1.x property names (correct for its pinned Maven 3.9.16/resolver 1.9.27) but resolver 2.0 (Maven 4) renamed both — the config silently becomes a no-op the day the wrapper moves to Maven 4, with no error at all.", "Maven Resolver's own docs explicitly reject the 'MD5/SHA-1 are insecure' framing for Maven: checksums provide integrity only, never security/trust/MITM protection — the Gradle-verification-metadata mental model doesn't transfer.", "`smartChecksums` only ever validates SHA-1 via an HTTP ETag header shortcut — leaving it on with a SHA-512-first algorithm list configures a stronger digest that never actually gets exercised; disabling it (as assertj does) is the operative half of the config, not the algorithm list.", "Maven has no equivalent of Gradle's verification-metadata.xml at all — no file, plugin, or built-in feature plays that role; the closest levers (resolver checksum properties, GPG signing) are orthogonal, not substitutes.", "apache/maven's own PR fixing the JDK17+ --add-opens/JaCoCo argLine clobber (apache/maven#11605) is still open and unmerged as of 2026-09-12 — the brief's cited bug has a proposed fix, not a shipped one.", "The one wrapper-checksum exemplar in the whole corpus (assertj) sets neither wrapperSha256Sum nor distributionSha256Sum — 0/1, not partial adoption."]}]}, {"group": "platform-and-toolchains", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-platform-and-toolchains.md", "must_count": 22, "conflicts_resolved": ["Timezone pinning severity: map row M-Q-06 ranks it P0/P1 while the encoding dive keeps it CONSIDER. Resolved CONSIDER — priority ranks research value, severity ranks what fails a build, and 0/32 corpus support cannot carry a MUST (jvm-quality-gates.md verdict 10 names this exact rule as the one agents invent).", "JAVA-PLAT-01's verification: the jdk-floors dive wrote it as a grep-and-flag on sourceCompatibility/<source>/<target>. That flags guava, whose 1.8 base layer is CORRECT as the base of a two-execution MR-JAR. Resolved: the check reads the owning compile task/<execution> for a governing release value — the same correction wave-2 #33 applied to GRADLE-DEP-05.", "Adversarial-locale CI job: the encoding dive rated it SHOULD (2/32). Demoted to CONSIDER with a reason the dive did not give — the MUST that actually catches locale defects is the call-site lint (-Xep:StringCaseLocaleUsage:ERROR, DefaultLocale) owned by JAVA-LINT; the CI job is the residual.", "jvmTarget vs options.release: the jdk-floors dive owns the Java floor knob and the Kotlin dive owns jvmTarget, and neither states the joint rule for a mixed Java+Kotlin module. Resolved into one rule (KT-COMP-04): the two are one decision written twice, with jvmTargetValidationMode (default ERROR) as the enforcement, plus the explicit caveat that a Kotlin-only module never triggers the validator.", "Kotlin floor, two conflated facts: frame correction 2 says Kotlin 2.4.0 ends K1 outright; the Kotlin dive cites whatsnew23 that -language-version 1.8/1.9 was dropped in 2.3.0. Both true and different — language-version values died at 2.3.0, the K1 compiler at 2.4.0. KT-COMP-07 requires a claim to name which.", "Archive-reproducibility scope: the encoding dive proposed DET-05/DET-06 as rules that mostly point at GRADLE-CACHE-17. Resolved: they are not rules in this family. JAVA-PLAT keeps only what Gradle's archive defaults cannot reach (Properties.store(), Maven outputTimestamp, mergeServiceFiles()'s classpath ordering); kafka's live opt-out is restated as evidence, not re-ruled.", "japicmp rule ownership: the jdk-floors dive proposed JAVA-PLAT-08/09 (bind to check/verify; flat accepted-breaks file). Those belong to JAVA-API/JAVA-LINT, already consolidated in wave 2. Resolved: the six-repo re-measurement and the copyable templates are handed back as evidence rather than duplicated as JAVA-PLAT rules.", "NullAway jspecifyMode rule ownership vs JAVA-LINT-05 in jvm-quality-gates.md: resolved by splitting on who breaks it — JAVA-LINT-05 owns the lint wiring, JAVA-PLAT-03 owns the toolchain-and-vendor precondition, because the agent editing the toolchain block is the one who trips it.", "Kover check wiring: the Kotlin dive settled that check DOES depend on koverVerify by default since 0.7.0 (opposite of JaCoCo), proven by a task tree and a maintainer's onCheck=false fix, while Kover's own current docs still never state it. Resolved as a correction handed to JAVA-TEST-08, not as a KT-COMP rule, to avoid a duplicated rule across families.", "Dokka's home: the Kotlin dive rates it a KT-COMP rule while M-Y-06 says docs-quality's generator table has no shape for Dokka. Resolved by splitting — the version-and-DSL rule is KT-COMP (it edits *.gradle.kts); only the unsolved tested-examples/generator-row gap goes to the sibling set as a file edit."], "needs_another_round": ["Mixed-module bytecode floor (KT-COMP/JAVA-PLAT boundary) — in a multi-project library, what mechanically enforces one bytecode floor across a Kotlin-only module and a Java-only module whose compile tasks never meet, given jvmTargetValidationMode only fires when both exist in the same module?", "Dokka in docs-quality (M-Y-06) — does Dokka get a new row in the sibling set's wiring-by-generator.md and tested-examples-by-language.md, or does the JVM set carry its own subprocess-harness rule for tested doc examples? Sibling-file edit either way, not a new artifact.", "Properties.store() on the Gradle side — is there any supported Gradle-side mechanism equivalent to Maven's java.properties.date, or must every plugin writing .properties output re-implement Shadow's ReproducibleProperties?", "JEP 472 JNI deny-by-default — has a target JDK release been announced since 2026-09-12? JDK 24 ships warn and the JEP promises only 'a future release', so no rule may name a number today.", "CI-runner timezone audit (JAVA-PLAT-17's decider) — do the CI runners the corpus actually uses default to UTC? That measurement is what separates '0/32 because nobody needs it' from '0/32 because nobody looked', and it is the only thing that can move the rule off CONSIDER in either direction."]}, "rule_id_count": 31, "dives": [{"slug": "jdk-floors-modules-and-removed-apis", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-platform-and-toolchains/jdk-floors-modules-and-removed-apis.md", "sources_count": 22, "surprises": ["Of the six declared japicmp adopters, only three (maven, gradle/gradle, testcontainers-java) bind the checker into the default build lifecycle — grpc-java's own build-file comment says 'Run with: ./gradlew japicmp --continue' (manual only), assertj only breaks the build inside a dedicated GitHub Actions workflow, and micronaut-core's check-binding lives in an external plugin repo not present in the sparse checkout", "micronaut-core's accepted-breaks file is a much lighter template than gradle/gradle's: a flat {type, member, reason} JSON array (1370 lines) consumed via the japicmp-gradle-plugin's own richReport{addViolationTransformer(...)} extension point, not a custom ViolationRule class hierarchy — but the transformer class itself still has to be hand-written, it isn't shipped by the plugin", "Because the OCX SDK's floor is already 17 (≥9), it needs none of guava's multi-release-JAR complexity for module-info.java — that indirection is strictly a floor-below-9 problem", "gradle/gradle's own Kotlin-DSL build is the SequencedCollection worked example, not a satisfier of any rule: gradle/gradle#27699 is still open with no code fix, only 'avoid the colliding name' as workaround", "NullAway's jspecifyMode JDK-vendor split is sharper than a version check: JDK 22+ works on any vendor including Oracle, but below 22 the flag is OpenJDK-family only (Temurin/Zulu) and literally does nothing on Oracle JDK 17/21 — and NullAway ≥0.12.11 throws IllegalStateException rather than degrading silently", "JEP 493 (jlink without JMODs) is confirmed a JDK-vendor build-time opt-in (--enable-linkable-runtime), not a JDK-24+ guarantee — jlink --help's own enabled/disabled line is the only way to know"]}, {"slug": "encoding-locale-timezone-determinism", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-platform-and-toolchains/encoding-locale-timezone-determinism.md", "sources_count": 16, "surprises": ["Shadow's ShadowJar never overrides Gradle's createCopyActionExecuter(), so Gradle 9's reproducibleFileOrder guarantee reaches shadowJar output for free, upstream of Shadow's entirely custom CopyAction/ZipOutputStream implementation — the two mechanisms (Gradle-level order, Shadow-level timestamp) are separate and answer the wave-2 handover question directly", "Shadow already ships its own fix for the exact Properties.store() timestamp-comment bug this dive was asked to flag at the JDK level (internal/ReproducibleProperties.kt: overrides store() to throw, sorts entries, exposes writeWithoutComments()) — proof the bug is real and a template for the fix, found in a repo nobody expected to cite for this", "mergeServiceFiles() merges into a Kotlin mutableSetOf() (LinkedHashSet), so merged META-INF/services line order is classpath-resolution order, not alphabetically sorted — Shadow's own docs don't state this either way, it required reading ServiceFileTransformer.kt directly", "JEP 400's own text states 'no intent to deprecate or remove' charset-less overloads — the fix is entirely in Charset.defaultCharset()'s value, meaning new String(byte[])/getBytes() are just as charset-less and just as lint-worthy on JDK 25 as on JDK 8, despite usually being 'correct' now", "kafka's build.gradle:362-364 copies Gradle's own pre-9 legacy-revert snippet verbatim while running wrapper 9.7.1 — an active, uncommented total opt-out of reproducibility that also silently negates whatever shadowJar in that same repo would otherwise inherit for free", "Maven's Reproducible-Builds-by-default only starts at 4.0.0-beta-5, and Central's newest 4.x artifact is still 4.0.0-rc-6 as of 2026-09-12 — so 'Maven 4 does this automatically' is a claim about an unreleased line, not the corpus's actual Maven baseline"]}, {"slug": "kotlin-toolchain-and-codegen", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-platform-and-toolchains/kotlin-toolchain-and-codegen.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["Kover's own DSL reference (0.9.8) genuinely never states the check/koverVerify default anywhere — the definitive answer came from a closed GitHub issue's literal Gradle task-dependency tree plus a maintainer's onCheck=false reply, not from any doc sentence.", "kapt in the exemplar corpus (google/dagger) is not legacy cruft being phased out — it's a deliberately preserved regression-test fixture (javatests/artifacts/**) proving the old Android/Hilt kapt path still works, while the real library modules are already KSP-only.", "ktorio/ktor's own gradle.properties contains an in-repo comment explaining a genuine Kotlin-daemon-precedence gotcha: -Dkotlin.daemon.jvm.options nested inside org.gradle.jvmargs governs the buildSrc daemon specifically because that daemon starts before the plain kotlin.daemon.jvmargs property is read.", "jvmDefault's default is now ENABLE (not just renamed from -Xjvm-default) per the live gradle-compiler-options.html table — confirmed independently by two exemplars (shadow, detekt) explicitly overriding it back to NO_COMPATIBILITY, implying real projects are already compensating for the flip.", "WebFetch's small-model summarization of kotlinlang.org pages was unreliable for flag-name precision (e.g. mangled '-Xwhen-guards' into '-Xguard-conditions' in one summary) — cross-checking whatsnew21 vs whatsnew22 vs the live compiler-reference.html was necessary to get exact flag identity right.", "extraWarnings/-Wextra has now sat at Experimental for four consecutive minor releases (2.1.0 through 2.4.0) with zero stabilization signal in any fetched whatsnew page — longer than typical for a Kotlin experimental feature, worth flagging as possibly stalled rather than just 'still cooking'."]}]}, {"group": "java-runtime-safety", "consolidation": {"path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-java-runtime-safety.md", "must_count": 15, "conflicts_resolved": ["errors dive §13 vs landed JAVA-LINT-10 on whether bare SpotBugs earns a gate slot — resolved by splitting per family: find-sec-bugs is required for JAVA-SEC (core SpotBugs has zero XXE/command-injection/path-traversal/JNDI detectors), while core SpotBugs CORRECTNESS+MT_CORRECTNESS independently earns a slot for UL_UNRELEASED_LOCK and the annotation-free NP_* family; JAVA-LINT-10's text is amended to add the second clause. Evidence: spotless runs Error Prone with disableAllWarnings=true + 5 checks and SpotBugs at Confidence.LOW.", "errors dive §13 vs jvm-audit/exemplar-quality-gates.md headline counts — resolved in the dive's favour (read-the-file beats grep-the-repo): Error Prone 13/32 corrected to 11/32 (kafka's build.gradle:2522 and maven's pom.xml:370,550 hits are error_prone_annotations *exclusions*, no plugin applied), SpotBugs-as-gate 3/32 corrected to 2/32 (assertj's block is <reporting>-only, no CI job runs mvn site or spotbugs:check).", "SEC dive §10 (\"bare SpotBugs contributes nothing\") vs ERR dive §13 (\"skip find-sec-bugs, SpotBugs earns its slot\") — apparent conflict only; dissolves once scoped by family, stated explicitly because the two sentences read as contradictory when quoted out of scope.", "ERR dive decision (a) — five JAVA-ERR rows in the always-loaded index — vs the 114-229-line index budget across nine families. Cut to three (ERR-01 re-interrupt, ERR-03 try-with-resources, ERR-04 chain the cause) plus JAVA-SEC-01's single reserved line; ERR-02 and ERR-05 drop to the depth file as reviewer-facing rather than every-edit.", "JAVA-ERR-06's verification (-Xlint:removal) vs landed JAVA-LINT-07, whose enumerated -Xlint key list omits `removal`. Resolved: add `removal` to that list, deliberately excluding `deprecation` as noisy. Raised as an owner decision because it edits a landed rule.", "Map row M-N-06 assigns ExecutorService shutdown to JAVA-CONC while the ERR dive covered it — resolved by splitting at the AutoCloseable seam: the JDK-19 close()-has-no-timeout trap stays here as JAVA-ERR-05, pool sizing and virtual-thread regime stay with JAVA-CONC; each rule's verification names the other.", "Map row M-O-04's three-peer resource-leak framing (S2095, PMD CloseResource, Error Prone) vs SonarSource's own record that S2095 deprecated pmd:CloseResource in Sonar 5.3, with PMD enforced 0/32 — resolved to two peers; naming PMD would route an agent to a superseded, unused tool.", "SEC dive decision (c) — the OCX SDK's subprocess is two boundaries, not one: inside the deserialization boundary (the ocx CLI is a thin client over a live service, so JAVA-SEC-02 binds its JSON handling) and outside the command/path boundary (the SDK is the producer, so JAVA-SEC-06 binds its ProcessBuilder call). Adopted as Verdict 3.", "SEC dive decision (b) — whether Oracle's Secure Coding Guidelines section 9 may be cited at all, given JEP 486. Resolved: citable only through Oracle's own historical-scope note; sections 0/1/2/3/5/8 stay current, and JAVA-SEC-09 makes proposing SecurityManager/doPrivileged/AccessController/.policy for JDK 24+ a MUST-not."], "needs_another_round": ["find-sec-bugs maintenance and signal-to-noise — is find-sec-bugs still maintained against SpotBugs 4.9+ and JDK 25 bytecode, and what is its false-positive rate on the XXE_* and PATH_TRAVERSAL_* families? JAVA-SEC-08 recommends a tool with 0/32 corpus adoption; if it is unmaintained, six of nine JAVA-SEC rules lose their only automatable gate and collapse to grep, which changes their severity, not just their verification column.", "Kotlin's half of this family (KT-ERR, or KT-CORO absorbing it) — neither dive covered Kotlin and JAVA-ERR's depth file does not load on **/*.kt. runCatching swallows CancellationException (a worse JAVA-ERR-01), use {} is the try-with-resources analogue, and with no checked exceptions JAVA-ERR-09's contest is void while @Throws placement replaces it. Does kotlin-quality need a KT-ERR depth file, or does KT-CORO absorb cancellation and KT-INTEROP absorb @Throws?", "Trust boundary for a subprocess with no live upstream — Verdict 3 is derived for an ocx CLI that is a thin client over a live service. Does the OCX Gradle plugin's `ocx --format json inspect --closure` read, which may run entirely against a local OCX_HOME cache, sit on the same side of the line as the SDK's, or does the dive's own \"re-derive, don't copy\" instruction put it outside?"]}, "rule_id_count": 18, "dives": [{"slug": "untrusted-input-and-deserialization", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-java-runtime-safety/untrusted-input-and-deserialization.md", "sources_count": 17, "surprises": ["Oracle's own seccodeguide.html has already been edited for JDK 24: all 20 Access Control guidelines (9-1..9-20) carry a verbatim 'security manager permanently disabled since Java 24' note — the source resolves its own currency problem, it doesn't need to be excluded wholesale.", "CVE-2026-54512 and CVE-2026-54513 are two distinct bypass mechanisms in one advisory (generic-parameter substring check vs allowIfSubTypeIsArray() component check), not one bug — patching one code path does not close the other; only a version bump fixes both.", "There is no CERT rule numbered specifically for JNDI lookups; the closest (IDS54-J) only covers the LDAP-query half. The operative primary sources are Error Prone's BanJNDI and CERT/CC's VU#930724, not a SEI IDS-category rule.", "Core SpotBugs has zero detectors for XXE, command injection, or path traversal, and only an indirect one (DMI_RANDOM_USED_ONLY_ONCE, which catches per-call Random instantiation, not security misuse) for the Random shape — the coverage gap is total, not partial, for 3 of the 6 core shapes.", "The exemplar corpus's own gradle/gradle build-logic contains an unguarded ObjectInputStream.readObject() call (SimpleClassMetaDataRepository.java) with no ObjectInputFilter — low risk (reads a locally self-written cache) but the exact unguarded shape this family flags, inside a flagship repo.", "The OCX SDK's subprocess-JSON boundary splits in two: inside the untrusted-input boundary for Jackson polymorphic-deserialization shapes (the CLI relays live-service data), but outside it for JNDI/ZipSlip/path/command shapes (the SDK is the producer, not consumer, of those operations) — it is not one blanket verdict."]}, {"slug": "errors-resources-and-lifecycle", "path": "/home/mherwig/dev/grimoire-lore/.agents/worktrees/cmake/.agents/research/jvm-java-runtime-safety/errors-resources-and-lifecycle.md", "sources_count": 19, "surprises": ["Of the six named checks, four are already ENABLED_ERRORS/ENABLED_WARNINGS by default (TryFailThrowable, MustBeClosedChecker, InterruptedInCatchBlock, StreamResourceLeak) — only CatchingUnchecked and UnusedException are truly DISABLED_CHECKS; the brief's framing overstated the off-by-default problem.", "assertj/assertj's SpotBugs declaration lives only under Maven's <reporting> block and no CI workflow ever runs `mvn site` or `spotbugs:check` — it never gates a build, contradicting wave-1's '3/32 SpotBugs-as-gate' count and mirroring the PMD performative-declaration pattern the brief already flagged elsewhere.", "apache/kafka's and apache/maven's 'uses Error Prone' hits in the wave-1 audit are false positives: both only exclude the transitive error_prone_annotations dependency, neither applies the net.ltgt.errorprone plugin at all.", "SonarJava S4507 (successor to deprecated S1148 for printStackTrace) reports as a Security Hotspot, not a standard Issue — a default Issues-only SonarQube quality gate shows zero findings for it even when printStackTrace is everywhere, per a confirmed SonarSource community thread.", "SpotBugs UL_UNRELEASED_LOCK — named explicitly in the brief — has no Error Prone equivalent anywhere in ENABLED_ERRORS/WARNINGS/DISABLED_CHECKS, and it fires on real production code (kafka's BufferPool.allocate()), making it concrete proof the SpotBugs residual set is non-empty.", "Error Prone already ships EmptyCatch, CatchAndPrintStackTrace and CatchFail as on-by-default ENABLED_WARNINGS — three checks not named in the brief that directly cover 'swallowed exception' and 'printStackTrace over logger' shapes better than the tools the brief pointed at."]}]}]

